File Name: HandRenderer.py

Necessary Packages: PySide6.Qt3DCore, PySide6.Qt3DExtras, PySide6.Qt3DRender, PySide6.QtGui

Necessary Modules (files): None

Overview: This file manages our live animation and our live animation window. It is accessed from GloveMonitorWindow.py.
It both creates the animation window and performs all updates on our animated object.

Methods: All methods are kept under the AnimationWindow class

__init__: creates all necessary components of our visual window. Our camera faces the +y direction relative to animated
events, with the back of the hand near the origin and the tips of the fingers pointed along the y-axis

_setup_camera: sets up the camera. This includes setting the window size ratio (16x9), camera position inside of the
window, the direction the camera points, the 'up' direction (positive z-axis), and settings for zooming and moving the
view inside the frame.

_setup_material: sets material color and determines how light interacts with our material

_setup_lighting: determines light source position and brightness. 2 total sources (above and below hand), with
the top light source being twice as bright as the lower one.

_add_point_light: determines color of light. Light is created relative to hand object.

_build_scene: calls methods to build components of hand to be animated (i.e. fingers)

_make_segment_entity: generates a single finger segment of a specified length, at a specified distance and angle
relative to a specified parent element

_build_palm: Creates our palm/wrist/back-of-hand (all same thing) object. This is the root object that operates a
reference point for position and behavior of all other objects within the animation frame.

_build_three_segment_finger: calls _make_segment_entity three times (once for each segment) to create a
3-segment finger object. parent object will always be our palm, but may be otherwise specified.

_build_four_fingers: builds four fingers off our palm parent entity. Exact sizing of finger segments may be edited here,
though I believe my selected proportions are reasonable.

_build_thumb: builds a thumb object onto our palm parent entity. It contains 2 segments and offset to the
right side of the palm.

_apply_flex: applies data from our flex sensor position (stored in HandKinematics) to the live animation view.
Essentially just rotates middle (center) segment relative to proximal (nearest to palm) segment, then rotates
distal (end) segment relative to middle segment.

setAnglesPointer, setAnglesMiddle, setAnglesRing, setAnglesPinky, setAngleThumb: From _apply_flex, sets (relative)
angles of middle and distal segments (just distal for thumb)

setOrientationPalm: Sets the palm rotation based on gyroscope sensor live position
(calculated and stored in HandKinematics.py). Before making changes, ensure basic understanding of quaternions.

BASIC IDEA: our gyroscope sensors do not track live position. Instead, they track angular velocity in relative
x, y, and z axes. This means that if our desire is to interpret angular position, we must create some summation of
these angular velocities (integral of speed) relative to our sampling frequency.

HOWEVER, whenever we rotate the sensors, the angle from which we are adjusting our angular position changes.
As a basic experiment, follow these steps imagining a sensor on the palm of your hand tracking relative angulation:
NOTE: you do NOT need to wear the glove for this experiment.
1. place your hand flat on the table. lift it up slightly
2. note that FROM THIS FLAT SET POINT, rotating your hand forward or backward results in sensor data in the +-y direction,
tilting your hand side-to-side results in sensor data in the +-x direction, and pivoting your hand (move from elbow) so
that your fingers point to the right or left (or backwards if you turn around 180 degrees) results in movement in the
+-z direction.
3. Now, consider a feature of properly tracking finger movement: if you start your hand in a position and stop your
hand in that same physical position, the total summation of your angles must be 0. You should get a result that
is 0 degrees x, 0 degrees y, and 0 degrees z.
4. Let's perform an experiment: start by placing your hand flat on a table, and then lift it up a bit.
5. rotate your wrist so your fingers point straight down. The sensors (correctly) track this movement as +90 degrees
around the y-axis.
6. Now, leaving your wrist static (preserve the 'L' shape), rotate your arm such that your fingers point to the left.
The sensors track this movement as + 90 degrees around the z-axis
7. Leaving your arm in the same position, point your wrist (and fingers) straight forward again. This is tracked as
-90 degrees around the y-axis.
8. Rotate your arm such that your hand is flat again. Go ahead and set it down on the table before. The sensor would
track this as -90 degrees around the x-axis.
9. Now, let's sum our total angle: +90Y + 90Z - 90Y - 90X = +90Z - 90X
What happened?! Our angle should have summed to 0, but now we have massive angle components in the x and z directions.
10. This is because our relative axis changes at the end of every rotation. In this program, we utilize quaternions to
simulate tracking our live axis and updating our angles accordingly.

This is not an explanation of how quaternions work, but is instead an example for a baseline intuition as to why they
are needed for this program. DO NOT ATTEMPT TO IMPLEMENT MATHEMATICS BASED ON EULER ANGLES. FOR THE LOVE OF ALL THAT
IS GOOD AND RIGHT (I phrase it this way because I am religious).

setOrientationFingers: Sets proximal segments relative to palm segments using Quaternion angle calculations
